WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.070
As a very general overview,

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:04.680
when we're talking about
training for hypertrophy,

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:06.120
we're typically looking at something

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:08.640
like 8 to 12 repetitions per set.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:12.090
Now, in the last five to eight years,

00:00:12.090 --> 00:00:14.460
it has become extremely clear,

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:16.740
hypertrophy happens pretty equally

00:00:16.740 --> 00:00:18.900
from as low as five repetitions per set

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.240
all the way up to 30 repetitions per set.

00:00:21.240 --> 00:00:22.980
So you can go anywhere in that window

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:24.990
as long as you're training
to the same effort

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:26.970
and expect equal hypertrophy.

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.590
But that said, most of the time,

00:00:28.590 --> 00:00:32.370
most people stick closer to
kind of 8 to 12 repetitions,

00:00:32.370 --> 00:00:35.400
but by all means, go lower or higher

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.220
and it should be no problem at all,

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.140
absolutely equally effective.

00:00:40.140 --> 00:00:42.630
What that means then in terms of intensity

00:00:42.630 --> 00:00:45.840
is that will scale based upon
how heavy you're lifting.

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:46.770
Generally, if you're in

00:00:46.770 --> 00:00:48.540
the kind of 8 to 12 repetition range,

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.720
you're probably in the 70 to 80%

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:53.550
of your one-rep max range.

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.720
If you were to go to a little bit lower,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:58.140
say five, six repetitions per set,

00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:00.720
you might be a little bit
higher, maybe 80, 85%.

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:01.560
And if you're to go lower,

00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:03.270
in fact, you can go as low as 30%

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:04.920
of your one-repetition maximum.

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:06.660
That's been shown
extensively in the research,

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:07.950
equally effective,

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:10.440
but that would just
require you to do 25 or 30

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:13.200
or potentially even more
repetitions per set.

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.000
I've talked about the
pros and cons of this

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:15.833
in other episodes

00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:19.230
and potentially will even next
season go into the details.

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.940
They are not the same

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.070
because of things like strength.

00:01:23.070 --> 00:01:26.010
So while you'll expect equal hypertrophy

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.530
with, say, 25 repetitions per set

00:01:28.530 --> 00:01:31.110
as you would with eight
repetitions per set,

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:34.080
the strength progress
will not be the same.

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:36.600
Strength needs to happen
at a lower rep range

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:39.600
so that you can get the intensity
of the load high enough.

00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:42.570
Typically what we're looking
at there is, of course,

00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:43.680
what I've spoken of before,

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:45.570
the three to five method,

00:01:45.570 --> 00:01:47.580
so three to five repetitions per set,

00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.440
but that could go a little bit higher.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.950
Really do remember, these
are not hard cutoff lines.

00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:56.130
These are just kind of phased out.

00:01:56.130 --> 00:01:59.190
So if I can get strength at
five repetitions per set,

00:01:59.190 --> 00:02:01.230
what do you think happens at six?

00:02:01.230 --> 00:02:02.190
Still some strength.

00:02:02.190 --> 00:02:03.023
What about seven?

00:02:03.023 --> 00:02:03.960
Yeah, still some.

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:05.430
Eight? Yeah, sure.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:06.480
But what about at 30?

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.590
Well, okay, very little.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.330
And so what happens is
it's just kind of like

00:02:09.330 --> 00:02:11.010
where is the general place to be

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:12.810
most of the time?

00:02:12.810 --> 00:02:14.520
That's all we're saying with strength.

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:17.220
But because of what we're trying to do,

00:02:17.220 --> 00:02:18.810
reach a high load,

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:21.270
we have to have a low repetitions per set

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:24.000
because fatigue starts to reduce

00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:25.680
the amount of weight we can actually lift.

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:27.300
So strength is generally something, again,

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:30.210
like three to five
repetitions, plus or minus.

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:31.530
Can be as low as one.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:33.330
If you want to truly peak strength,

00:02:33.330 --> 00:02:36.000
you do one repetition as heavy as you can.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:37.980
There's cons to that as well.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:39.120
You want to go a little bit higher,

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:41.460
five, six, seven reps,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:42.720
okay, still fine,

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:44.610
but maybe you have to
go a little bit lighter.

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:46.860
Gives you rough context
of what you're looking at.

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:51.000
For power, just continue
that logic one more step.

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.320
So because power has a strength
and velocity component,

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:58.290
not only can I not afford
to lose some force output,

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:00.780
I can't afford to lose velocity either.

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:05.280
And so power happens typically
in a similar rep range,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:06.570
almost always though less

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:10.470
than kind of six reps per set at the most,

00:03:10.470 --> 00:03:12.360
usually even less than that, right?

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.510
Two to five, probably more
so even than strength,

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:17.400
and we have to do it at a fast speed.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.600
So the intensity,

00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.240
the load can't be as high

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:22.920
'cause I have to move it fast, okay?

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.870
So while you could do something like this,

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:27.480
say three sets of three repetitions

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:30.780
at 95% of your bench press for strength,

00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:33.030
that's going to move very slow,

00:03:33.030 --> 00:03:35.310
and so that wouldn't be
great at developing power.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:36.240
We would want to go to maybe

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.360
three sets of three repetitions at 65%.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:41.100
So not super, super light,

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.380
'cause now we're just
working on pure speed,

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.060
but not super heavy either

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:47.790
because now we're just
working on pure force.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:49.470
Somewhere in the middle.

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:54.300
Every exercise has a
different optimal load,

00:03:54.300 --> 00:03:55.920
in other words, how heavy you can lift,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:59.520
what percentage of your
one-rep max to maximize power.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:01.800
Generally, very generally,

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:04.740
upper body smaller exercises

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:08.220
tend to achieve their peak
power at a lighter percentage,

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:10.290
40% of your max, 50% of your max,

00:04:10.290 --> 00:04:12.600
maybe as low as 30% of your max.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:15.210
Bigger exercises like a squat,

00:04:15.210 --> 00:04:18.300
higher, maybe 50% of your
max, 60% of your max.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:19.770
More complicated movements

00:04:19.770 --> 00:04:22.350
like a snatch or a clean and jerk

00:04:22.350 --> 00:04:26.280
can even be as high as like
80 to 95% of your one-rep max.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.750
So it does vary a little bit,

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:29.880
but the point with power always is

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:34.140
it has to be a combination
of heavy and fast.

00:04:34.140 --> 00:04:36.930
If it's fast and no load,

00:04:36.930 --> 00:04:38.180
it's speed but not power,

00:04:39.030 --> 00:04:41.130
which is okay too if
that's what you're after.

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:44.400
If it's heavy and very, very slow,

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:47.700
it's force, which is okay,
but it's not power either.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:50.160
Power needs to be somewhere in the middle.

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:51.510
So with that in mind, then,

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.990
the rest range for hypertrophy

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.670
can really be whatever you'd like.

00:04:56.670 --> 00:04:59.190
It can be as small as 30 seconds.

00:04:59.190 --> 00:05:01.770
Now, by doing that, you're going
to accumulate more fatigue,

00:05:01.770 --> 00:05:04.710
which means you're either going
to have to do less repetitions

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.650
or less weight or a combination,

00:05:07.650 --> 00:05:09.270
but that's absolutely fine.

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:11.250
It can be high rest.

00:05:11.250 --> 00:05:12.660
Even up to five minutes of rest

00:05:12.660 --> 00:05:16.530
have been shown to produce
equal hypertrophy as 30 seconds.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:19.860
Obviously, if you go more
rest, you get more recovery,

00:05:19.860 --> 00:05:20.730
which means we now need

00:05:20.730 --> 00:05:23.400
to keep the weight high or go higher

00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:25.860
and keep the rep range high or go higher.

00:05:25.860 --> 00:05:27.900
And so pros and cons, there
are subtle differences,

00:05:27.900 --> 00:05:31.350
but equal benefit from short rest ranges

00:05:31.350 --> 00:05:32.280
as low as 30 seconds

00:05:32.280 --> 00:05:35.640
all the way up to longer rest
ranges up to five minutes.

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:37.650
For strength and power though,
it's a different story.

00:05:37.650 --> 00:05:39.900
You need to stay away for the most part

00:05:39.900 --> 00:05:42.000
of low rest intervals.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:45.660
Remember, this is all about quality.

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:48.630
We need to practice moving
fast a bunch of times.

00:05:48.630 --> 00:05:52.080
We need to practice moving
heavy a bunch of times.

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:55.890
If we shorten our rest intervals
and we can't move as fast

00:05:55.890 --> 00:05:58.470
and we can't produce as much force,

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:01.230
then we're not practicing producing force

00:06:01.230 --> 00:06:04.530
and we're not practicing moving faster,

00:06:04.530 --> 00:06:07.620
and so we're really
undercutting our adaptations.

00:06:07.620 --> 00:06:09.660
Two to five minutes of rest

00:06:09.660 --> 00:06:13.263
is the rough recommendation
for power and strength,

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:16.440
but you do want to think
about this in context.

00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:18.963
If I were to do, say, one vertical jump,

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:22.530
I don't truly need to rest five minutes.

00:06:22.530 --> 00:06:23.910
That's not that fatiguing.

00:06:23.910 --> 00:06:26.100
I could probably honestly do one minute

00:06:26.100 --> 00:06:28.620
and depending on how fit you are,

00:06:28.620 --> 00:06:31.470
you might even be able to do,
like, 40 seconds and be fine.

00:06:31.470 --> 00:06:33.480
And so they're just guidelines, okay?

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:34.710
Think about the person,

00:06:34.710 --> 00:06:36.570
think about what phase
of training they're in.

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.580
Early in a training phase,

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:40.320
maybe you want more rest,

00:06:40.320 --> 00:06:43.710
but 16 weeks into a training program,

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:46.710
you're very fit, you're very
strong, you're in shape,

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:48.060
you maybe don't necessarily need

00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:50.250
a full extensive rest interval

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.170
to produce peak power again, okay?

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:53.610
If you're trying to maximize,

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:55.050
truly maximize your strength,

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.020
you're probably going to take
five-plus minutes of rest.

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.540
If you're trying to work on a
lot of different repetitions,

00:07:00.540 --> 00:07:02.760
on a lot of exercises of power,

00:07:02.760 --> 00:07:04.410
maybe two minutes is good.

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:06.667
Five, oftentimes when I
say that people are like,

00:07:06.667 --> 00:07:08.730
"Oh my god, five whole minutes?"

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.320
And they go to the gym, they do it,

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:12.420
they start their clock
and like 30 seconds in

00:07:12.420 --> 00:07:13.830
they're like, "Oh my gosh.

00:07:13.830 --> 00:07:16.500
Like, I have to wait
another 4 1/2 minutes?"

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:19.230
Well, not necessarily all the time

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:21.720
and not for every exercise.

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:23.043
I will caution though,

00:07:24.390 --> 00:07:28.200
you are not nearly as
recovered as you think you are.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:29.700
If you're used to doing things

00:07:29.700 --> 00:07:31.320
that are like moderate strength

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.540
or hypertrophy or muscular endurance,

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:34.890
you're not really understanding

00:07:34.890 --> 00:07:36.660
and you typically can't register

00:07:36.660 --> 00:07:39.660
what it feels like to be at 100%.

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:41.647
You get to kind of 90, 92% and you think,

00:07:41.647 --> 00:07:44.280
"Yeah, yeah, I don't feel any difference."

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:45.420
In this particular case,

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:48.840
if we're trying to maximize
power and strength,

00:07:48.840 --> 00:07:51.570
that last 7% matters,

00:07:51.570 --> 00:07:52.740
but you may not be able to perceive it

00:07:52.740 --> 00:07:53.910
if you're not used to it.

00:07:53.910 --> 00:07:55.140
And so typically what I would say

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:57.630
is if you're not used to
this type of training,

00:07:57.630 --> 00:07:59.040
I would still take more rest intervals

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:00.930
than you think you want to.

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:01.763
What's the harm?

00:08:01.763 --> 00:08:03.780
You cost yourself an extra six minutes

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:04.980
combined at the end of the workout

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.560
by doing 20 more seconds of rest?

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:09.720
Okay, you can live with that, okay?

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:11.160
If eventually you realize that's enough

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:13.140
and you want to back it
back down, that's great,

00:08:13.140 --> 00:08:15.930
but initially start off with
more rest than you think.

00:08:15.930 --> 00:08:16.920
For the most part,

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:18.990
people err on the side of too short

00:08:18.990 --> 00:08:21.510
when it comes to power and strength,

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:22.760
not necessarily too long.

00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:24.760
[music]

